  "interpretation": [
    {
      "heading": "The plain baseline",
      "body": "The fixed code does not move plain DreamerV3: 4.77 against 4.63, well inside the noise of a 30-world score (about \u00b10.55 between world sets; a gap under ~1.1 between single runs is not evidence). Both stay at the wood tier: no stone is collected in any episode, and lifespans (265-295 steps) sit near the random-policy band (261-284)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "The next agent with the rule mask is far above the baseline",
      "body": "next_lam0 scores 11.20 \u00b1 0.41 against plain's 4.77 \u00b1 0.28, and 11.30 \u00b1 0.27 on 60 worlds. It makes the stone sword in 50% and the stone pickaxe in 30% of episodes on worlds 0-29, which no plain run does. But the rule mask reads the game's action rules, so this run is counted as cheating and does not rank against honest agents. Without the mask the same family scored 6.03 \u00b1 0.33 for manager v1.2 (v12_nomask, family/v) and 5.77 for RSSM-2 plus the manager without curiosity (abl_manager); both are on other branches."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What would settle the rest",
      "body": "next_nomask (the next agent without the rule mask) scores 5.63 \u00b1 0.27 on 30 worlds and 5.82 \u00b1 0.17 on 60 (rerun 2026-11-09 00:22-17:42 CST, after the first attempt was killed by the box's out-of-memory crash). That is level with v12_nomask (6.03; 6.13 on 60 worlds), about one achievement above plain (4.77), and 5.5 below next_lam0 (11.30 on 60 worlds): without the mask the next agent's parts add nothing over manager v1.2. It dies young (mean life 249 steps; 17 of 30 deaths from thirst). next_notaste crashed three times on GPU faults (Xid 69, 13) and has no result; next_lam0 stands in for it, since taste at lam_mgr 0 adds exactly zero. Taste on the manager (next_taste, family/taste) scores 9.80 \u00b1 0.42 against next_lam0's 11.20 on the same worlds, so taste hurt; see docs/taste-on-manager.md."
    }
  ],
